Our container images have grown to the point where the registry's storage bill is being questioned, and the platform team traced most of the growth to build context bloat rather than to the compiled artifacts themselves. Three services share a common Docker build pattern: the build context is the repository root, and the Dockerfile copies the whole tree before running the package manager. That pattern sends node_modules, local build output, test fixtures, documentation screenshots, and editor backups into the builder, even though the image only needs the application source and the lock file. The platform team asked service owners to add a .dockerignore file at the root of each repository and gave a short list of patterns to include: the dependency directory, the build output directory, coverage reports, log files, the .git directory, and editor and operating system metadata such as .DS_Store and swap files. They also want a one-line comment at the top explaining why the file exists, so that future contributors do not delete it thinking it is stale. The repository in question already has a Dockerfile at the root and a node_modules directory that is currently being copied into images; there is no .dockerignore yet, and creating one will not affect any build until the next image build is triggered, which is a separate manual step. I am not asking to change the Dockerfile itself in this step, and I am not asking to rebuild anything. The next step is to create the ignore file with the agreed patterns. The content should list node_modules, dist, coverage, *.log, .git, .DS_Store, and *.swp, each on its own line, under the explanatory comment. The path is the repository root, which is /home/dev/webapp. This operation only creates a new text file; nothing is overwritten. Return the single tool call that performs the next step
